New opt-in option `--modular-termination` (experimental; it does not yet establish termination in every program).  With it, `decreases {:global} E` declares a method's or function's global bound.  A call dispatched to a bodyless member of a trait whose implementations may lie in other modules is rejected unless the callee's global bound is below that of the member making the call, and a call to known code that can reach such a dispatched call is rejected unless the callee's global bound is at most the caller's, so a helper that forwards a dispatched call needs a bound of its own.  Code without a bound that can reach such a call (a subset type's or newtype's constraint or witness, the default values of a type's components, or a constant's initializer) counts as called wherever a member mentions the type or reads the constant, so a member with a bound may not do so; before, a type test could run such a constraint unchecked and prove `false`.  A call through a function value whose code is not known has no bound either, so a member with a bound may not make it, and `decreases {:callback g} E` gives an in-parameter `g` of an arrow type the bound `E`: an application of `g` must be below the caller's bound, and the value passed for `g` must be a lambda expression, whose body is then checked under `E`, a parameter with a callback bound that is not larger, or a function that reaches no dispatched call.  The option does not yet establish termination in every program: a library's traits are open only by the extensions it sees, and the comparison of bounds may use the definitions of the functions whose termination it establishes.  With `--show-hints`, it reports every call that needs one of the rules.
